  "body": {
    "id": "prop:hybrid-expected-risk-upper",
    "kind": "proposition",
    "statement": "For every \\(n,m\\ge2\\), the hybrid estimator \\(\\widehat U^{\\mathrm H}\\) is a member of \\(\\mathfrak E_{n,m}\\).  There is a universal constant \\(C<\\infty\\) such that\n\\[\n \\sup_{P\\in\\mathfrak P^{\\mathrm{pred}}_{n,m}(r,\\mu,K,q,B,\\lambda)}\n E_P\\!\\left[\\mathcal L(\\widehat U^{\\mathrm H},U)\\right]\n \\le C\\{1\\wedge a^{\\mathrm{loose}}_{nm}\\}.\n\\]\nThe estimator is the single data-driven map in \\(\\mathrm{def:hybrid\\mbox{-}estimator}\\); it is not a law-dependent oracle choice.",
    "free_symbols": [
      "n",
      "m",
      "UhatH",
      "Eobs",
      "P",
      "r",
      "mu",
      "K",
      "q",
      "B",
      "lambda",
      "L",
      "U",
      "aloose"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "def:predictable-class",
      "def:rowwise-loss",
      "def:observed-estimators",
      "def:loose-radius",
      "def:hybrid-estimator",
      "prop:ipw-expected-risk-upper",
      "lem:discrete-sine-fallback-bound"
    ],
    "proof_tex": "The recorded score is a measurable function of \\((A,Y,p)\\), and the rank projection with its fixed tie rule is measurable.  Thus \\(\\widehat U^{\\mathrm I}\\) is \\(\\mathcal O_{n,m}\\)-measurable.  The fallback \\(U^{\\mathrm F}\\) and the predicate \\(a^{\\mathrm{loose}}_{nm}\\le1\\) are deterministic functions of the fixed dimensions and envelopes.  The two-branch definition therefore makes \\(\\widehat U^{\\mathrm H}\\) \\(\\mathcal O_{n,m}\\)-measurable.  Both branches lie in \\(\\mathbb O_{n,r}\\), by the definition of \\(\\widehat U^{\\mathrm I}\\) and Lemma \\(\\mathrm{lem:discrete\\mbox{-}sine\\mbox{-}fallback\\mbox{-}bound}\\).  The definition of \\(\\mathfrak E_{n,m}\\) consequently gives\n\\[\n \\widehat U^{\\mathrm H}\\in\\mathfrak E_{n,m}.\n \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe radius \\(a^{\\mathrm{loose}}_{nm}\\) is deterministic and positive because \\(n,m\\ge2\\), \\(B>0\\), \\(q>0\\), \\(\\lambda_{nm}>0\\), \\(r\\ge1\\), and \\(\\mu\\ge1\\).  If \\(a^{\\mathrm{loose}}_{nm}\\le1\\), the hybrid equals \\(\\widehat U^{\\mathrm I}\\), and Proposition \\(\\mathrm{prop:ipw\\mbox{-}expected\\mbox{-}risk\\mbox{-}upper}\\) gives\n\\[\n \\sup_P E_P[\\mathcal L(\\widehat U^{\\mathrm H},U)]\n \\le C_1a^{\\mathrm{loose}}_{nm}\n =C_1\\{1\\wedge a^{\\mathrm{loose}}_{nm}\\}.\n \\tag{2}\n\\]\nIf \\(a^{\\mathrm{loose}}_{nm}>1\\), the hybrid equals \\(U^{\\mathrm F}\\).  Every law in the predictable class satisfies left incoherence, so the pathwise fallback lemma and \\(\\mu\\ge1\\) yield\n\\[\n \\mathcal L(\\widehat U^{\\mathrm H},U)\n \\le1+\\sqrt{2/\\mu}\n \\le1+\\sqrt2\n =(1+\\sqrt2)\\{1\\wedge a^{\\mathrm{loose}}_{nm}\\}.\n \\tag{3}\n\\]\nTaking expectations and the supremum over the same class preserves (3).  Equations (2)--(3), with \\(C=\\max\\{C_1,1+\\sqrt2\\}\\), prove the asserted uniform risk bound.  Since the branch is selected only by a deterministic radius, (1) also proves that the witness is one admissible data map rather than a per-law family.",
    "justification": "The proposition exhibits one measurable estimator attaining \\(C(1\\wedge a^{\\mathrm{loose}}_{nm})\\) uniformly over the unchanged class.",
    "gap": "The constant endpoint need not be minimax optimal when \\(b_{nm}\\to0\\).",
    "consumer": "Trial analysts receive an explicit estimator whose normalized maximum-row risk never diverges.",
